og of inputsqrt(in)	.	.	.	.	.	square root of inputexp(in)	.	.	.	.	.	.	e to the power of the inputcpsmidi(in)	.	.	.	.	.	convert Hertz to MIDI note numbers	Input is a frequency in cycles per second (Hertz) and will be converted to a MIDInote number. This note number is a floating point value, which means that the integerpart is the MIDI key number and the fractional part times 100.0 is the number of centssharper than the MIDI key number for the input frequency.midicps(in)	.	.	.	.	.	convert MIDI note numbers to Hertz	Takes a MIDI key number and converts it two cycles per second (Hertz). The reverseof the above function.	Oscillators :oscil(table, freq, phase)	.	table lookup oscillator with freq and phase modulation	This is the standard table lookup oscillator. 'table' is a wavetable. 'freq' is afrequency modulation input in cycles per second. 'phase' is a phase modulation inputin radians.oscili(table, freq, phase)	.	interpolating oscillator with freq and phase modulation	This is the same as oscil with the addition that the output samples are linearlyinterpolated from the wavetable, reducing noise due to table lookup.poscil(carfrq, modfrq, index)	phase modulation oscillator pair	A single unit generator that contains a pair of sine oscillators, one phase modulating the other. 'carfrq' is the carrier frequency in Hertz. 'modfrq' is themodulator frequency in Hertz. 'index' is the phase modulation index in radians. (For those used to frequcy modulation, this is the same as the modulation index forfrequency modulation, which is also in radians, by the way, and produces the sameoutput spectrum as frequency modulation.)poscili(carfrq, modfrq, index)	interpolating phase modulation oscillator pair	Same as above but using linear intepolation of the sine table.	pmoscil(carfrq, modfrq, index)	phase modulation oscillator pair using 68881 sin function	Same as above but uses the 68881 sin function instead of interpolating. It turnsout that this function is faster than the above, so is always preferred over poscili,since it is much more accurate (== less noisy).	coscil(table, frq, beatfrq)	.	detuned pair of oscillators	Implements a detuned pair of oscillators and is exactly equivalent to :		oscil(table, frq + beatfrq/2, 0) + oscil(table, frq - beatfrq/2, 0)		but easier to type.	coscili(table, frq, beatfrq)	interpolating detuned pair of oscillators	Same as coscil with linear interpolation.sinosc(freq, phase)	.	.	.	sine wave oscillator using 68881 sin function	A sine wave oscillator using the 68881 sin function.	soscil(table1, table2, fm1, fm2)	.	hard sync oscillator with window functionvosim(table, frq, wavefrq)	.	VOSIM-like oscillatorControl Functions :sinlfo(freq, phase)	.	.	.	sine lfolfo(table, freq, phase)	.	.	table lookup lfotriang(freq, amp)	.	.	.	triangle wave lfosquare(freq, amp)	.	.	.	square wave lfo  (-amp..+amp)square0(freq, amp)	.	.	.	square wave lfo  (0..+amp)saw(freq, amp)	.	.	.	.	sawtooth wave lfopulse(freq, pwm, amp)	.	.	variable pulse width lfo  (-amp..+amp)pulse0(freq, pwm, amp)	.	.	variable pulse width lfo  (0..+amp)blip(freq, amp)	.	.	.	.	one sample impulse wave lforamp(start, end)	.	.	.	ramp generatoradsr(lvl1, lvl2, t1, t2, t3, t4)	old analog 4 stage exponential envelope generatorar(lvl, t1, t2)	.	.	.	.	.	old analog 2 stage exponential envelope generatorlinen(lvl, t1, t2, t3)	.	.	linear flat top envelopelinseg(lvl1, lvl2, lvl3, t1, t2, t3, t4)	.	.	.	.	4 stage linear envelopelinseg0(lvl0, lvl1, lvl2, lvl3, lvl4, t1, t2, t3, t4)	.	4 stage linear envelopesah(in, trigger)	.	.	.	sample and holdgate(in, trigger)	.	.	.	gate or holdtrig(in)	.	.	.	.	.	schmidtt triggerlag(in, time)	.	.	.	.	exponential decaylag2(in, atktime, dcytime)	.	exponential attack and decayRandom Generators :noise0(freq, amp)	.	.	.	random noise with no interpolation - "buzzy"	Steps to random values every int(1/freq) samples. Sounds "buzzy" or "raspy".	noise1(freq, amp)	.	.	.	random noise with linear interpolation - "fizzy"	Ramps linearly to next random value every int(1/freq) samples. More band limitedthan noise0. Sounds "fizzy".	noise(freq, amp)	.	.	.	random noise with quadratic interpolation - "whirry"	Swerves parabolically to next random value every int(1/freq) samples. More band limited than noise1. This is the best noise source to use in most cases. Sounds"whirry".wavelets(wavtbl, density, ctrfreq, bw)	.	.	.	.	wavelet generatorgrains(wavtbl, amptbl, dur, density, ctrfreq, bw)	.	granular synthesis generatorFilters :diff(in)	.	.	.	.	.	derivative of inputinteg(in)	.	.	.	.	.	integral of inputsum(in)	.	.	.	.	.	.	two point sum of inputavg(in)	.	.	.	.	.	.	two point average of inputflip(in)	.	.	.	.	.	inverts spectrum about SampleRate/4flipsum(in)	.	.	.	.	.	inverts spectrum about SR/4, then notches out SR/2tone(in, cfreq)	.	.	.	.	simple first order lowpass filteratone(in, cfreq)	.	.	.	inverse of tonereson(in, cfreq, bw)	.	.	simple second order resonant filterareson(in, cfreq, bw)	.	.	inverse of resonlpf(in, cfreq)	.	.	.	.	Butterworth low pass filterhpf(in, cfreq)	.	.	.	.	Butterworth high pass filterbpf(in, cfreq, bw)	.	.	.	Butterworth band pass filterbrf(in, cfreq, bw)	.	.	.	Butterworth band reject filterAmplitude Functions :rms(in)	.	.	.	.	.	.	rms of input signalgain(in, level)	.	.	.	.	sets rms of input to levelbalance(in, control)	.	.	balances rms of input to control signalshaper(in, tbl)	.	.	.	.	nonlinear transfer functionpan(in, pos, outL, outR)	.	stereo panner - assigns panned input to output L & Rpanx(in, pos, outL, outR)	.	stereo panner - mixes panned input to output L & Rxfade(in1, in2, mix)	.	.	cross fade between two inputsDelays :delay1(in)	.	.	.	.	.	one sample delaydelay(in, time)	.	.	.	.	delay linedelayi(in, time)	.	.	.	interpolating delay lineflange(in, time, mix)	.	.	flangerflangei(in, time, mix)	.	.	interpolating flangercomb(in, time, rvt)	.	.	.	comb delay lineech